  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a source\n\n- $y=2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?",
  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/d03ab1d15be3656787d7ccd0114b2011f561bfed9c67d22f3366f50ed2f410e4.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/890c699885c8694597912bd65044fde50dd99ac8757578dc0db71bdb413b5db7.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.",
